<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · c. 380 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Te Deum</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Latin hymn of praise about 1,600 years old, still sung in churches to give thanks for good news such as peace or a royal birth.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous (traditionally ascribed to Saint Ambrose or Saint Augustine)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous (Gregorian chant)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Latin Christians</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>not known (Western Roman Empire; spread with the Ambrosian Rite of Milan)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>4th century; traditionally before AD 500</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words anonymous, 4th century (traditionally ascribed to Ambrose or Augustine))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/te-deum">Te Deum</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Christian hymn of praise, named from its opening words. Tradition ascribes it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Saint Ambrose</a> (died 397) or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Saint Augustine</a> (died 430), and it is sometimes called the Ambrosian Hymn, though Ambrose's authorship is unlikely. Scholars of the 19th century proposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilary-of-poitiers">Hilary of Poitiers</a> and Nicetas of Remesiana; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> from the 10th century on link the hymn with the name Nicetas, a case defended in the 1890s by Germain Morin, but hymnologists of the 20th century, especially Ernst Kähler (1958), showed that link to be spurious. The hymn almost certainly dates from the 4th century and its author is unknown. Its structure resembles a eucharistic prayer, and it has been proposed that it began as part of one.</p><p>The hymn was central to the Ambrosian hymnal, which spread through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Church</a> with the rite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> between the 6th and 8th centuries. It entered the Benedictine order's Old Hymnal in the 6th century and was kept in the Frankish Hymnal of the 8th, dropped from the New Hymnal that prevailed from the 10th century, and restored in the 12th. In the traditional office it is sung at the end of Matins on Sundays and feasts outside the penitential seasons; since the reforms of Pope Paul VI it closes the Office of Readings on most Sundays, solemnities and feasts. It is also sung in Anglican Morning Prayer, by Lutherans, Methodists and many Reformed churches.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/te-deum">Te Deum</a> is the core of a short service of thanksgiving, often held at short notice for good news: a military victory, the signing of a peace treaty, a royal birth or coronation, the election of a pope or the canonisation of a saint. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> such a service may be held in the Royal Chapel for royal births and jubilees; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> one is held every 21 July, the national day, the date on which Leopold I officially became king of the Belgians; in Luxembourg one marks the Grand Duke's Official Birthday; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, Chile and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> hold one on their national days.</p><p>The hymn was first sung to a Gregorian chant melody, and many composers have set it since: Lully, who was fatally injured while conducting his setting for the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, Charpentier, Handel, Haydn, Mozart, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hector-berlioz">Berlioz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Verdi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-bruckner">Bruckner</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonin-dvorak">Dvořák</a> and Britten among them. The prelude of Charpentier's setting H.146 became the theme music of the European Broadcasting Union's Eurovision broadcasts. William Walton wrote his Coronation <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/te-deum">Te Deum</a> for the coronation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> in 1953, and Puccini set its opening at the end of Act I of Tosca. The 18th-century German hymn Großer Gott, wir loben dich is a free version of it, sung in English as &quot;Holy God, We Praise Thy Name&quot;.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of praise that follows the outline of the Apostles' Creed. It names all who praise God, from the ranks of heaven to the Church on earth, recalls the life, death, resurrection and glory of Christ, and ends with petitions for mercy and protection drawn from the Psalms, which were added to the original hymn later.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Latin</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Latin</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Te Deum laudámus:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You, God, we praise:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">te Dóminum confitémur.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you, Lord, we acknowledge.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Te ætérnum Patrem,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You, the eternal Father,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">omnis terra venerátur.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>all the earth worships.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Tibi omnes ángeli,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To you all the angels,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">tibi cæli</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to you the heavens</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et univérsæ potestátes:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and all the powers:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">tibi chérubim et séraphim</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to you cherubim and seraphim</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">incessábili voce proclámant:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with unceasing voice cry out:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Sanctus, Sanctus, Sanctus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Holy, Holy, Holy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Dóminus Deus Sábaoth.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lord God of Sabaoth.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Pleni sunt cæli et terra</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Full are the heavens and the earth</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">maiestátis glóriæ tuæ.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of the majesty of your glory.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Te gloriósus</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You the glorious</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">apostolòrum chorus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>choir of the apostles,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">te prophetárum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you the praiseworthy</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">laudábilis númerus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>number of the prophets,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">te mártyrum candidátus</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you the white-robed</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">laudat exércitus.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>army of martyrs praises.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Te per orbem terrárum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You throughout the circle of the lands</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">sancta confitétur Ecclésia,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the holy Church acknowledges,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Patrem imménsæ maiestátis;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Father of boundless majesty;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">venerándum tuum verum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your venerable, true</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et únicum Fílium;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and only Son;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Sanctum quoque</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and also the Holy</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Paráclitum Spíritum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Spirit, the Paraclete.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Tu rex glóriæ, Christe.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You, king of glory, O Christ.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Tu Patris sempitérnus es Filius.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You are the everlasting Son of the Father.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Tu, ad liberándum susceptúrus hóminem,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You, when you were to take up man to set him free,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">non horrúisti Virginis úterum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>did not shrink from the Virgin's womb.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Tu, devícto mortis acúleo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You, the sting of death overcome,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">aperuísti credéntibus regna cælórum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>opened to believers the kingdoms of the heavens.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Tu ad déxteram Dei sedes,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You sit at the right hand of God,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">in glória Patris.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in the glory of the Father.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Iudex créderis esse ventúrus.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You are believed to be the judge who will come.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Te ergo quǽsumus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We therefore ask you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">tuis fámulis súbveni,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>come to the help of your servants,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">quos pretióso sánguine redemísti.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>whom you have redeemed with precious blood.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ætérna fac cum sanctis tuis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Make them, with your saints, in eternal</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">in glória numerári.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>glory, to be numbered.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Salvum fac pópulum tuum, Dómine,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Make your people safe, Lord,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et bénedic hereditáti tuæ.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and bless your inheritance.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Et rege eos, et extólle illos</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And rule them, and lift them up</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">usque in ætérnum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>even unto eternity.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Per síngulos dies benedícimus te;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Through each single day we bless you;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et laudámus nomen tuum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and we praise your name</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">in sǽculum, et in sǽculum sǽculi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>into the age, and into the age of the age.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Dignáre, Dómine,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Deign, Lord,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">die isto sine peccáto nos custodíre.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>on this day to keep us without sin.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Miserére nostri, Dómine, miserére nostri.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Have mercy on us, Lord, have mercy on us.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Fiat misericórdia tua,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let your mercy be,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Dómine, super nos,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lord, upon us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">quemádmodum sperávimus in te.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>as we have hoped in you.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">In te, Dómine, sperávi:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In you, Lord, I have hoped:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">non confúndar in ætérnum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let me not be put to shame for ever.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional (4th century; Latin text as in the Vatican's edition). Text from <a href="https://la.wikisource.org/wiki/Te_Deum" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (13)</summary><ul><li>General: the accents in the Latin (laudámus, Dóminum) only mark stress for singing; they do not change the sense.</li><li>lines 1-4 and 14-21: the Latin puts 'Te' (you) first and the verb last; that order is kept, so 'You … the choir of the apostles, the number of the prophets, the army of martyrs praises' is one sentence over lines 14-19.</li><li>line 2: 'confitemur' means to acknowledge, confess or profess (as one's Lord).</li><li>line 7: 'potestates', the powers, are an order of angels.</li><li>line 11: 'Sabaoth' is a Hebrew word kept in the Latin: hosts, armies (of heaven).</li><li>line 18: 'candidatus', clothed in white (the white robes of the martyrs).</li><li>line 20: 'per orbem terrarum', literally 'through the circle of the lands', i.e. throughout the whole world.</li><li>line 26: 'Paraclete', from the Greek: advocate, helper, comforter.</li><li>line 29: 'susceptúrus hóminem', about to take on (human nature as) man; 'ad liberandum', in order to set free.</li><li>lines 39-40: one clause: 'make them be numbered with your saints in eternal glory'. 'Aeterna' agrees with 'gloria' on the next line. Older manuscripts read 'munerari' (be rewarded) where this text has 'numerari' (be numbered).</li><li>line 41: 'Salvum fac', literally 'make safe' = save.</li><li>line 47: 'in saeculum, et in saeculum saeculi', literally 'into the age, and into the age of the age': for ever and ever.</li><li>line 55: 'confundar', be confounded, put to shame, brought to ruin.</li></ul></details></section>
