<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Te Deum (Jean-Baptiste Lully)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical setting, for the court of Louis XIV</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Te Deum, H.146 (Marc-Antoine Charpentier)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical setting; its prelude became the Eurovision theme</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Te Deum, H.145, H.147, H.148 (Marc-Antoine Charpentier)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical settings</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Te Deum, S.32 (Michel Richard de Lalande)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Te Deum, two settings (Henry Desmarets)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1687</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical settings</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Te Deum, C.137, C.138, C.155 (Louis-Nicolas Clérambault)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical settings</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Settings by Zelenka, Handel (five English settings), Haydn, Mozart, Berlioz, Verdi, Bruckner, Furtwängler, Dvořák, Britten, Kodály and Pärt</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical settings</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">English settings by Thomas Tallis, William Byrd, Henry Purcell, Edward Elgar, Charles Villiers Stanford (three), Arthur Sullivan (two), Ralph Vaughan Williams (two), Herbert Howells and Richard St. Clair</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical settings</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Tosca (Giacomo Puccini), end of Act I</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the opening of the hymn set in the opera</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Te Deum organ works (Louis Marchand, Guillaume Lasceux)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">elaborate compositions on the chant melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Te Deum for organ (Charles Tournemire)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1930</span><span class="sg-chain-h">composition on the chant melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Te Deum for organ (Jean Langlais)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1934</span><span class="sg-chain-h">composition on the chant melody</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Book of Psalms</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the closing petitions are verses from the Psalms, appended to the hymn later</span></li></ul></section>
